# Tier hand-off doorbell (campaign tier-handoff-doorbell): decode MoE layers
# no longer stop the enqueueing thread. Strata's doorbell
# (src/kernels/cuda/elementwise.cu:186-311) and FreeToken's flag handshake
# (python/freetoken/moe/cpu_executor.py:31-49), on OpenCL.
#
# MOE_DOORBELL=1, decode calls of up to 63 pairs and 8 tokens on a partly
# resident pool with the CPU tier (patch 0076's served configuration):
#  - the enqueueing thread applies the ready swaps, fills a few never-filled
#    pinned slots, rewrites the layer's residency table (device memory, by an
#    enqueued copy, only when it changed), then enqueues without blocking:
#    doorbell_route (pair table from topk_id through the table: slot, or -1
#    for the CPU tier; publishes the ids, the slots it chose, the routing
#    weights and x to host memory, then release-stores the call's sequence
#    number), the batched expert kernels (which now skip -1 rows), the
#    shared-expert stages, doorbell_wait (polls the host's done flag, merges
#    the CPU rows into y), and the reduce. Then it pushes the host half to a
#    coordinator thread and returns: the whole decode step is submitted ahead.
#  - the coordinator (one thread, FIFO = layer order) spins on the published
#    sequence number, counts the ids for the adaptive cache, runs the CPU tier
#    on exactly the pairs the device marked -1, writes their rows and the row
#    list to host memory, and rings done as its last write.
#
# The GPU-side poll, measured on the B60 (xe, NEO; host USM reports no
# concurrent access): only an LSC load that bypasses L1 and L3
# (__builtin_IB_lsc_load_global_uint, L1UC_L3UC) WITH an acquire fence inside
# the loop sees the host's write (8 of 8, 4.5-5.4 us); acquire atomics 2 of 8,
# volatile and RMW loads 0 of 8, and the bare uncached load is hoisted out of
# the loop. OpenCL user events were not used: a command waiting on one puts
# the queue in blocked mode and moves later submissions onto the thread that
# sets it.
#
# Failure handling: the poll is bounded (~1.3 s); a timeout, or a host half
# that throws (it still rings done, with a poison word), makes the wait kernel
# write NaN rows instead of merging, and the error is thrown on the next call.
# A coordinator job for a layer superseded by a later published call throws.
# The enqueueing thread drains the coordinator before touching an impl whose
# last call has not rung, and the impl's destructor drains it. First use
# (the host bank, its fill, the lookahead's routers, host scratch) happens on
# the enqueueing thread, never inside a job the GPU waits on.
#
# Served gate (dense d48q8, B60, ratio 75 + census128, 15.4e9 device pool,
# 30 GiB fixed bank, adaptive cache, router lookahead; 20,085-token needle;
# one fresh process per arm): without the doorbell prefill 63.5 / needle
# decode 10.7 / 500-token decode 14.0 t/s; with it 60.8 / 12.7 / 16.0; needle
# right in both. Window-0 KL over three arms each: 0.409 vs 0.412 nats mean,
# argmax 79.1 vs 79.0 % (one arm pair alone read +0.038, inside the control's
# own 0.041 spread). Device timeline before (CLIntercept, 2k): busy 32.6 ms
# of ~70 ms a decode token.
#
# Files: moe_expert_swiglu.cl (the two kernels, the -1 skips),
# moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp (stages, coordinator, host half, wiring),
# expert_weight_providers.{hpp,cpp} (swap apply, table, fill, counting, the
# swap-list mutex), moe_otd_perf.hpp / moe_otd_runtime.cpp (counters), and the
# unit cell.

